Real DevOps Shell Scripts Use Cases:

✅ Automate Log Rotation:

#!/bin/bash
for file in /var/log/*.log; do
  mv "$file" "$file.$(date +%F)"
done

🎯 Goal: Renames all .log files in /var/log/ by appending today’s date to their filenames (log rotation by date).
🔎 Breakdown:
•#!/bin/bash — Shebang, script uses Bash.
•for file in /var/log/*.log; do — Loop through every .log file in /var/log.
•mv "$file" "$file.$(date +%F)" — Rename each file by appending today’s date (YYYY-MM-DD format).
•Example: syslog.log → syslog.log.2025-05-09
•done — Ends the for loop.

✅ Monitor Disk Usage and Alert:

#!/bin/bash
usage=$(df / | grep / | awk '{print $5}' | sed 's/%//')
if [ $usage -gt 80 ]; then
 echo "Disk usage is above 80%!" | mail -s "Disk Alert" admin@example.com
fi

🎯 Goal: Checks root (/) disk usage, and sends an email alert if it's over 80%.
🔎 Breakdown:
•usage=$(...) — Captures disk usage percentage (just the number).
-df / — Shows disk usage for /.
-grep / — Filters the line for /.
-awk '{print $5}' — Extracts the 5th column (e.g., 85%).
-sed 's/%//' — Removes the % sign.
•if [ $usage -gt 80 ]; then — If usage > 80%, trigger alert.
•echo "Disk usage..." | mail -s ... — Sends an email with subject "Disk Alert".
•You need mailutils or similar package installed for mail to work.

💭 What is a Pipe (|) in Shell?
A pipe is used to connect the output of one command as the input to another.
🧠 Think of it as: "Take the output from the left side and send it as input to the right side."

✅ Backup Script:

#!/bin/bash
backup_dir="/backup"
mkdir -p $backup_dir
tar -czf $backup_dir/home_$(date +%F).tar.gz /home

🎯 Goal: Creates a compressed backup of the /home directory with today's date in the filename.
🔎 Breakdown:
•backup_dir="/backup" — Set the target backup folder path.
•mkdir -p $backup_dir — Creates the directory if it doesn't exist.
•tar -czf ... — Compresses the /home folder into a .tar.gz archive:
-c = create
-z = compress with gzip
-f = specify filename
•Filename: home_YYYY-MM-DD.tar.gz using $(date +%F)
•Example: home_2025-05-09.tar.gz
